INT. CALIFORNIA ZEPHYR - DINING CAR - NIGHT - 1953

White linen, silver, a vase with a single carnation on each table. The window shows a dark prairie slipping by under a quarter moon. A pianist in the corner plays something soft.

LUCILLE MERCER, 30s, in an emerald dress and long gloves, sits alone at a table for two, a cocktail untouched at her elbow. Across the aisle, HENRY BOYLAN, 30s, in a grey suit with a loosened tie, reads a menu he is not seeing. A WAITER named ARMAND, 50s, white jacket, pencil behind his ear, hovers between them. At the far table, BELINDA CROSS, 30s, in sapphire, sips champagne and watches them both over the rim of her glass.

Calm. Murmurs. The clink of cutlery.

ARMAND
(to Lucille)
Your companion has not arrived, madam?

LUCILLE
He's late.

ARMAND
Shall I bring the soup?

LUCILLE
In a moment, Armand.

Henry glances up. Lucille's eyes meet his across the aisle. He looks away.

ARMAND (CONT'D)
(to Henry)
And you, sir? The sole?

HENRY
Please.

LUCILLE
Henry.

Henry lowers the menu.

HENRY
Lucille.

LUCILLE
I didn't think you'd come.

HENRY^
I didn't think you'd come.

They stop. Lucille laughs, a small helpless sound. Henry shakes his head and gets up. He crosses the aisle, steadies himself against the lurch of the train, and sits opposite her. Belinda lowers her glass. Armand discreetly withdraws a step and examines the carnation.

LUCILLE (CONT'D)
You look well.

HENRY
You look better.

LUCILLE
Armand, a second place, please.

ARMAND
Of course, madam.

Henry lifts her cocktail, sniffs it, grimaces, and sets it down.

HENRY
Still gin and nothing.

LUCILLE
Still a coward.

HENRY
Still right.

Belinda rises. Smooths her gown. Crosses to their table with a smile that doesn't reach her eyes.

BELINDA
Henry, darling. You promised me the first dance.

Lucille's gloved hand closes on the stem of her glass.

HENRY
Belinda. This is Lucille.

BELINDA
Of course it is.

LUCILLE
We've met.

BELINDA
In Chicago. At the hospital benefit.

LUCILLE
You wore sapphire then, too.

BELINDA
It suits me.

Henry stands. He moves between them as the train lurches into a curve, and the carnation vase slides off the table and shatters.

ARMAND
(from a distance)
I shall fetch a broom.

Henry looks at the broken glass. Then at Lucille, and Belinda, and the carnation on the floor.

HENRY
I should have written.

LUCILLE
You should have.

BELINDA
He has been writing me for a year.

Lucille slowly turns her head. Belinda is smiling now, genuinely, as though she has been waiting for this moment since Chicago.

LUCILLE
(quietly)
Henry. Is that true?

Henry opens his mouth. The train whistle shrieks. The pianist, at last, stops playing.
